{
    "background_story": "Lea, a young and devout Muslim, grew up in a multicultural community where she was fascinated by the stories of her grandparents, who had immigrated from Iran. Every year, during the month of Muharram, Lea's family would gather to commemorate the martyrdom of Imam Hussein, the grandson of the Prophet Muhammad. Lea was particularly drawn to the story of Hussein's bravery and sacrifice, and she spent hours listening to her grandfather's tales of the Battle of Karbala. As she grew older, Lea became increasingly involved in organizing Muharram events in her community, using her talents to create powerful artwork and poetry that reflected the themes of justice, compassion, and selflessness. Her dedication and creativity inspired others to learn more about the significance of Muharram, and soon, Lea's community was coming together to honor the values of Imam Hussein and promote unity and understanding.",
